EIGHT THUMB BIT OPERATIONS TRANSLATED UNTIL THE FIRST UNGRANTED INSTRUCTION.

A bottom-up machine-code-to-English Major C specimen.
This is NOT an English Major C executable; it starts from actual
assembler-produced ARMv7-A Thumb instruction halfwords
and conservatively stops when the declared permission set ends.

SOURCE AND PROVENANCE OF THE INSTRUCTION BYTES.

Diagnostic source assembly used:
  .syntax unified
  .arch armv7-a
  .thumb
  .text
  .thumb_func
  rev r2, r1
  rev16 r3, r4
  revsh r5, r6
  rbit r7, r8
  bfc r3, #8, #8
  bfi r3, r2, #8, #8
  ubfx r4, r5, #4, #7
  sbfx r6, r7, #2, #6
  teq r1, r2
  orn r3, r4, r5
  rrx r6, r7

The production diagnostic employed
  clang 17.0.0, --target=armv7a-none-eabi, -mthumb;
  llvm-objdump 17.0.0, --triple=thumbv7-none-eabi.
The diagnostic assembled source in a disposable
working directory and inspected the resulting
ELF32 little-endian ARM object file.
These are FOREIGN DIAGNOSTIC TOOLS ONLY,
not the established maintained ICK/NDK
build route for English Major C.

The independently decoded instruction list was:

  offset 0x0000  halfword BA0A        REV R2,R1
  offset 0x0002  halfword BA63        REV16 R3,R4
  offset 0x0004  halfword BAF5        REVSH R5,R6
  offset 0x0006  halfwords FA98 F7A8 RBIT R7,R8
  offset 0x000A  halfwords F36F 230F BFC R3,#8,#8
  offset 0x000E  halfwords F362 230F BFI R3,R2,#8,#8
  offset 0x0012  halfwords F3C5 1406 UBFX R4,R5,#4,#7
  offset 0x0016  halfwords F347 0685 SBFX R6,R7,#2,#6
  offset 0x001A  halfwords EA91 0F02 TEQ R1,R2
  offset 0x001E  halfwords EA64 0305 ORN R3,R4,R5
  offset 0x0022  halfwords EA4F 0637 RRX R6,R7

The last three instructions are retained as binary
provenance but are NOT translated as successful
English Major C operations in THIS closed profile:
TEQ, ORN, and RRX remain outside the initial
72-operation grant. A future version may
admit them with separate exact long names.

The eight preceding operations are all permitted,
provided their normal T32 execution context
and executable memory mapping are valid.
The first three are TWO bytes each. Each
subsequent permitted operation occupies FOUR bytes.
The halfword at offset 0x0008 belongs to the
RBIT starting at 0x0006 and is NOT its own
sequential successor.

PART ONE. DECLARED INITIAL MACHINE STATE.

The abstract current instruction stream begins
at offset zero within the diagnostic code.
It is not an observed Android virtual address.

All register values used as source inputs
are explicitly declared before execution:

    R1 = 0x12345678.
    R4 = 0x12345678.
    R6 = 0x000080FF.
    R8 = 0x0000000D.
    R0, R2, R3, R5, R7, R9-R12 are initially unknown.
    SP and LR are initially unknown.
    N=1, Z=1, C=1, V=0.

The registers are 32-bit. The flags are the four
architecturally observable comparison flags.
The demonstration assumes that the entire
stated instruction-byte region can be fetched
and decoded in T32 state, that the selected
instructions are legal, and that no exception
or interrupt intervenes during the eight steps.

No data-memory content is required by
these eight register-only instructions,
and none of them performs a data access.

PART TWO. THE EIGHT SUCCESSIVE MACHINE OPERATIONS.

EXECUTION STEP ONE.

At relative execution index 0x0000,
fetch BA0A, a TWO-byte instruction.
Its permitted long name, operation 65,
starts "Reverse the positions of the four
successive eight-bit portions of a
selected thirty-two-bit general-register value".
The encoded source is R1, destination R2.
Copy byte 0x78 to the most significant
destination byte, then 0x56, 0x34, 0x12
to successively lower byte positions.
After the instruction:
    R2 = 0x78563412;
    R1 remains 0x12345678;
    N/Z/C/V remain 1/1/1/0;
    execution index = 0x0002.

EXECUTION STEP TWO.

At index 0x0002, fetch BA63,
a TWO-byte REV16 selecting R4 as source
and R3 as destination, operation 66.
The original R4=0x12345678.
Swap 0x12 with 0x34 within its high halfword;
swap 0x56 with 0x78 within its low halfword.
After the instruction:
    R3 = 0x34127856;
    R4 remains 0x12345678;
    all condition flags remain unchanged;
    execution index = 0x0004.

EXECUTION STEP THREE.

At index 0x0004, fetch BAF5,
a TWO-byte REVSH selecting R6 as source
and R5 as destination, operation 67.
The least significant sixteen source bits
are hexadecimal 0x80FF.
The two component bytes exchange places,
giving hexadecimal 0xFF80.
Because its new bit 15 is one,
fill bits 16 through 31 with ones.
After the instruction:
    R5 = 0xFFFFFF80;
    R6 remains 0x000080FF;
    all condition flags remain unchanged;
    execution index = 0x0006.

EXECUTION STEP FOUR.

At index 0x0006, the first halfword FA98
announces a 32-bit Thumb instruction. Fetch
the next halfword F7A8 as part of that
SAME instruction, operation 68, RBIT.
The source R8 is 0x0000000D.
Reversing the positions of all 32 bits
produces 0xB0000000.
After the instruction:
    R7 = 0xB0000000;
    R8 remains 0x0000000D;
    flags unchanged;
    execution index = 0x000A.
Do not invent an instruction at 0x0008.

EXECUTION STEP FIVE.

At index 0x000A, halfwords F36F 230F
specify operation 69, BFC with destination
R3 and a cleared interval from bit 8 to
bit 15 inclusive, total width eight.
The old destination R3 is 0x34127856.
Clearing the selected byte 0x78 yields:
    R3 = 0x34120056.
No other destination bit may change.
No memory changes, flags remain unchanged,
execution index = 0x000E.

EXECUTION STEP SIX.

At index 0x000E, halfwords F362 230F
specify operation 70, BFI. The current
old destination R3 is 0x34120056.
The current old source R2 is 0x78563412.
The eight least significant source bits
are hexadecimal 0x12. Put those into
destination bit positions 8 through 15.
The resulting register is:
    R3 = 0x34121256.
R2 remains 0x78563412.
The original R3 bits outside 8..15
are preserved. Flags unchanged.
Execution index = 0x0012.

EXECUTION STEP SEVEN.

At index 0x0012, halfwords F3C5 1406
specify operation 71, unsigned bit-field
extraction from R5 to R4 with first bit
4 and width 7. R5 is now 0xFFFFFF80.
The seven source bits in positions 4..10
produce the unsigned integer 0x78.
All destination bits 7..31 are set to zero.
After the instruction:
    R4 = 0x00000078;
    R5 remains 0xFFFFFF80;
    flags unchanged;
    execution index = 0x0016.

EXECUTION STEP EIGHT.

At index 0x0016, halfwords F347 0685
specify operation 72, signed bit-field
extraction from R7 to R6 with first bit
2 and width 6. R7 is 0xB0000000.
Bits 2..7 of R7 are all zero, so the
selected field has sign zero.
All higher destination bits are filled
with zero rather than one.
After the instruction:
    R6 = 0x00000000;
    R7 remains 0xB0000000;
    flags unchanged;
    execution index = 0x001A.

PART THREE. WHERE THIS TRANSLATION MUST STOP.

The bytes at offset 0x001A are EA91 0F02,
the 32-bit Thumb TEQ register instruction
according to the independent diagnostic decoder.

TEQ is listed in the separate coverage ledger
as UNGRANTED. It is NOT operation 16, TST:
testing an exclusive-or and testing an AND
are different bitwise predicates with
different condition-flag results.

The instruction stream does not disappear
because the language lacks an operation
name for it. Rather, the proposed language
MUST report:

    An encountered native instruction is not in
    the currently permitted operation set.
    The attempted translation is incomplete.
    The exact unsupported instruction begins at
    offset 0x001A and occupies FOUR bytes.
    No effects at 0x001A or later can be claimed
    under the current source operation grant.

Therefore the trace ends at 0x001A,
not at an invented function return.
The following ORN and RRX bytes remain
visible in the source-machine provenance
but are not silently interpreted.

This is a useful boundary test for the
concept "all and only the following operations."
It must not be weakened to "execute the instruction
anyway because the physical processor supports it."

PART FOUR. OBSERVABLE RESULT OF THE EIGHT PERMITTED STEPS.

The exact ordered execution-index sequence,
including the first unsupported boundary, is:

    0x0000
    0x0002
    0x0004
    0x0006
    0x000A
    0x000E
    0x0012
    0x0016
    0x001A (stop: ungranted TEQ)

After the eight permitted operations:
    R1 = 0x12345678.
    R2 = 0x78563412.
    R3 = 0x34121256.
    R4 = 0x00000078.
    R5 = 0xFFFFFF80.
    R6 = 0x00000000.
    R7 = 0xB0000000.
    R8 = 0x0000000D.
    N=1, Z=1, C=1, V=0.

All other register values are unknown,
not zero merely because they are absent
from this trace. Memory is unchanged.
No branch, call, store, or exception is
claimed within the eight specified steps.

PART FIVE. TWO NUMERICAL COUNTEREXAMPLES.

SIGNED VERSUS UNSIGNED EXTRACTION.

The program's actual sequential R7 value
produces zero for operation 72. That
coincidence is not sufficient to test its
sign extension. Supply a separate input
where R7=0x000000E0, with first bit 2
and width 6. The six selected bits
represent negative eight. SBFX must
produce 0xFFFFFFF8; UBFX would produce
0x00000038. The two are NOT interchangeable.
This is a separate model input and not
the preceding eight-instruction trace.

REVSH VERSUS ZERO EXTENSION.

With source low halfword 0x80FF, REVSH
produces 0xFFFFFF80. A zero-extending
interpretation would produce 0x0000FF80.
Such a result is wrong even though
the lowest sixteen bits agree.

PART SIX. REQUIRED INDEPENDENT CHECKS.

Positive: confirm all first and second
instruction halfwords match the diagnostic
object file, that the first three are
two bytes each, and that the next five
are four bytes each.

Positive: confirm eight allowed long
English operations map to numbers 65-72
without an unnamed intermediary opcode.

Positive: with the declared input state,
the eight result registers and four flags
match the exact output state above.

Negative: reject an incorrect translation
that treats second halfword F7A8 as the
instruction at index 0x0008.

Negative: reject a translation that changes
the flags when REV, BFC or SBFX executes.

Negative: reject an incorrect BFI that
uses the high byte of R2 rather than its
eight least significant bits.

Negative: reject an erroneous UBFX that
sign-extends source bit 10 instead of
zero-filling destination bits above bit 6.

Negative: reject an incorrect SBFX sign-bit
choice, especially when selected sign differs
from the original source bit 31.

Negative: reject continuing execution of
TEQ at 0x001A while claiming the profile's
closed set of 72 operations is respected.

Negative: reject counting the LLVM
assembler/disassembler diagnostic as
a complete language parser, certified
semantic execution or an Android build.

(NOTE:
    The diagnostic source's assembler mnemonics
    are provenance. The English Major C
    executable vocabulary uses long
    numbered names with separate definitions.
    One can retain hundreds of words of inert
    NOTES and WHY blocks without requiring the
    language's execution processor to interpret
    them as instructions.
)

(WHY? BECAUSE:
    The user's proposed method is bottom-up:
    start with the actual final machine instruction
    and describe its complete observable effect.
    A machine-level translation is not valid
    merely because it resembles a computation
    that could have been written in a high-level
    language. The fixed permission boundary
    must remain visible when an unfamiliar
    instruction is encountered.
)

PART SEVEN. EVIDENCE LEVEL.

The instruction byte and disassembly examples
were produced by a foreign assembler/decoder
for an explicitly selected T32 target,
and the expected arithmetic was independently
checked with ordinary bit calculations.
No ARM CPU executed those instructions
in this assignment.

No machine-code equivalence checker,
English Major C preprocessor, parser,
interpreter, compiler, or physical
device acceptance has been executed.

The constraints and alternatives remain
subject to ARM DDI 0406C.d and the
project's future independent acceptance.
